  The return value stays exactly as it is. I considered returning the record instead, but the store is the delivery
  mechanism and changing the return shape would be a breaking change bought for nothing.

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains one line: [38;5;153m<script src="identity.js"></script>[39m above the existing script tag.

  [1mSection 3 — Failure behavior[0m

  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m throws in real conditions — Safari private browsing, storage disabled by policy, quota exhaustion.
  Since attribution is non-critical, [1ma storage failure must never break login.[0m [38;5;153mset[39m catches, warns, and returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m;
  login succeeds regardless.

  [38;5;153mget[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m when nothing is stored [3mand[0m when the stored JSON is corrupt (clearing the bad key on its way out).
  Every consumer therefore has to handle [38;5;153mnull[39m. With attribution-only scope that branch is trivial — the form submits
  unattributed — but it exists at every call site, and future forms need to know that.

  [1mSection 4 — Testing, and a tooling question[0m

  The project has no test runner, no lint, and no format config. Since [38;5;153midentity.js[39m will be loadable in Node with a fake
  storage, unit tests are cheap here: Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m needs zero dependencies and no config.

  This is the cheapest moment to decide — before any code exists.

